WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.260
C++ binary interfaces can be fragile because the language gives interfaces many ways to expose implementation details.

2
00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:11.160
Public classes can expose object layout.

3
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:15.020
Templates can push implementation into every caller that instantiates them.

4
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.760
Inline functions can bake behavior into callers at build time.

5
00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:27.940
Standard library types may have different layout or behavior across compilers, library versions, debug modes, and build options.

6
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:34.440
Virtual hierarchies can become difficult to evolve when method order or object layout is part of the contract.

7
00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:45.040
A small private change may still affect binary callers if it changes object size, alignment, vtable layout, inline behavior, or exported symbols.

8
00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:53.220
Source code may continue to compile for new clients while existing clients built against the previous binary contract fail at runtime.

9
00:00:53.320 --> 00:01:01.480
This is why ABI review often looks at compiled artifacts, exported symbols, and layout assumptions rather than source headers alone.

10
00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:07.920
The defensive lesson is to keep binary interfaces narrow, stable, and intentionally designed.

11
00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:13.780
Avoid exposing fragile C++ details when long-term binary compatibility is required.

12
00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:23.800
Prefer interfaces that hide layout, reduce inline coupling, limit exported symbols, and clearly separate source-level convenience from binary-level commitments.

13
00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:31.500
Stable ABI design is less about avoiding all change and more about choosing which parts of the system are allowed to change without breaking callers.

14
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:37.680
That choice should be visible in headers, release notes, tests, and review checklists.

15
00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:44.040
One common pattern is to separate the pleasant C++ interface from the hardened binary boundary beneath it.

16
00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:52.300
Header-only helpers, wrappers, and RAII classes can improve source ergonomics while delegating across a smaller stable ABI.

17
00:01:52.400 --> 00:02:00.720
This lets teams offer a familiar developer experience without exposing every private type, template choice, or layout decision as a compatibility promise.
